{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments ["<b>וְלֹא יִזַּח הַחֹשֶׁן</b> — This is an expression of tearing loose (נִתּוּק), and it is an Arabic word, as Dunash ben Labrat says, following the language of Rashi (Rashi on Exodus 28:28). But it seems closer to me that it is like יִסַּח, as in 'The house of the proud YHWH will tear away' (Proverbs 15:25), 'and the treacherous shall be torn away from it' (ibid. 2:22), the meaning being that of uprooting and tearing loose (נְתִיצָה וְנִתּוּק), through the interchange of סמ\"ך and זי\"ן, as in 'let the devout exult (יַעְלְזוּ) in glory' (Psalms 149:5) and 'let us be intoxicated (נִתְעַלְסָה) with love' (Proverbs 7:18); 'and you shall tear down (וְנִתַּצְתֶּם) their altars' (Deuteronomy 12:3), 'they tore up (נָתְסוּ) my paths' (Job 30:13). And likewise 'and you shall keep the watch of the house מַסָּח' (2 Kings 11:6) — from the root of נָתַץ; he warns them that none of them should separate and tear up the guard. And perhaps also 'and she is girded with מֵזַח continually' (Psalms 109:19) — meaning destruction and tearing down (הֶרֶס וּנְתִיצָה): he says that the curse shall be girded about him continually until it destroys and tears him down by it, just as others gird themselves with their belts. 'אֵין מֵזַח עוֹד' (Isaiah 23:10) — there is no destruction anymore: he says to Tyre, cross over like the Nile to the land of Tarshish, which used to trade with you, and there is no destruction and separation anymore, for none of you shall be separated from there, nor shall you return to Tyre once it is utterly destroyed. One who is separated from the station of his companions is called 'הוֹרֵס' (one who breaks through), as in 'let them not break through (יֶהֶרְסוּ) to YHWH to see' (Exodus 19:24), and it is written 'from your station he shall tear you down (יֶהֶרְסֶךָ)' (Isaiah 22:19). And likewise 'and וּמְזִיחַ streams He loosens' (Job 12:21) — the tearing up of the streams, which are the flowing wadis that wash away and destroy mountains and valleys. And מֵזַח is weighed against מֵצַח, both having a נו\"ן [in their root], for it is from the root נִצּוּחַ, since the strength of the head is in the forehead (מֵצַח)."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:17:18.420040877Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}